#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# glaisher

#% text
# `glaisher` is Glaisher's constant `A = 1.2824...`, defined by `log(A) = 1/12 - zeta'(-1)`; it is the constant in the asymptotic product `1^1 2^2 ... n^n ~ A n^(n^2/2 + n/2 + 1/12) e^(-n^2/4)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `glaisher` is exact and known to be a positive real; `n(glaisher, d)` gives `d` digits, also inside larger expressions, up to about 60000 digits (10000 digits take some seconds).

#% item
# The digits come from `log(A) = (euler_gamma + log(2 pi))/12 - zeta'(2)/(2 pi^2)`, with `zeta'(2)` summed by the Euler-Maclaurin formula and a proved error bound.

#% item
# `zeta'(-1)` evaluates to `1/12 - log(glaisher)`, and `barnes_g` and `log_barnes_g` at half-integers give closed forms in `glaisher`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
glaisher
#% output
#= glaisher

#% cell
n(glaisher)
#% output
#= 1.2824271291006226

#% cell
n(glaisher, 50)
#% output
#= 1.282427129100622636875342568869791727767688927325001192

#% cell
[is_real(glaisher), is_positive(glaisher), 1 < glaisher and glaisher < 2]
#% output
#= [true, true, true]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative of the zeta function at -1:

#% cell
zeta'(-1)
#% output
#= 1/12 - log(glaisher)

#% text
# The Barnes G-function at 1/2 and its logarithm:

#% cell
barnes_g(1/2)
#% output
#= 2^(1/24)*exp(1/8)/(glaisher^(3/2)*pi^(1/4))

#% cell
log_barnes_g(1/2)
#% output
#= 1/8 - (3/2)*log(glaisher) - (1/4)*log(pi) + (1/24)*log(2)

#% text
# The hyperfactorial `1^1 2^2 ... n^n` divided by its asymptotic form tends to `A`:

#% cell
n(product(k^k, [k, 1, 200])/(200^(200^2/2 + 200/2 + 1/12)*exp(-200^2/4)), 20)
#% output
#= 1.28242717362918414122

